TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Preliminary Report (Part 1)","overview":"Plan a simple space mission that benefits Australia and present preliminary design work to guide the full mission assignment. Explain the mission concept and rationale, plan how the assignment will be completed, identify information sources and software, and show evidence of technical progress."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Mission concept and rationale — What will it do, who are the users, why is it needed?","weight":40,"max":40,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"A clear and complete description of mission goals, objectives, and requirements. Clearly relates required mission function to users and their needs. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Sufficient description of mission goals, objectives, and requirements. Identification of users and their needs. Descriptions feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"A poor description with mission goals, objectives and requirements unclear. Insufficient identification of users and their needs."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Project plan — How you plan to complete the assignment, including either a Gantt chart or a timetable and information sources you plan to use","weight":30,"max":30,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The plan for project completion is robust and has provision for project variations and contingencies. The plan fits within the narrative set out by the mission concept – the student makes clear why the plan is developed this way in the context of the mission. Appropriate references to information sources are included."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Plan is presented but lacks some detail. However, the plan is enough to believe that the project is feasible."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Plan is not included or does not have sufficient detail to demonstrate that the project can be completed."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Preliminary progress — Technical content and evidence of sufficient work done to date","weight":10,"max":10,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Good preliminary set of results included with brief description and/or interpretation. Clearly on track for completion."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Minor work completed on the project. Results description or interpretation may be insufficient."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful work completed."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":20,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical structure. The report is very easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Mission Concept & Rationale"},{"id":"R2","met":"partial","where":"Project Plan & Preliminary Progress"},{"id":"R3","met":"partial","where":"Image 1"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Image 1"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Project Plan & Preliminary Progress"},{"id":"R6","met":"no","where":null}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Project Plan & Preliminary Progress (Orbit Specifics)","issue":"A geostationary orbit is at about 35,786 km altitude over the equator, not 600 km. A 600 km orbit cannot remain fixed above the reef at 15–24°S, so the stated orbit is physically incorrect."},{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","issue":"A single GEO relay is not justified for near-real-time monitoring of the reef at useful resolution. The chosen orbit is inconsistent with the stated mission goal and no trade-off is shown."},{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","issue":"Ocean acidification is cited as the cause of bleaching, but bleaching is driven mainly by thermal stress. The 30% figure refers to a hydrogen-ion concentration increase and is not explained, so the causal link is overstated."},{"where":"Project Plan & Preliminary Progress","issue":"The text reserves weeks 7 and 8 for editing, but the Gantt chart schedules Editing & Finalisation in weeks 9 and 10. The schedule is internally inconsistent."},{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","issue":"The bathymetry claim is attributed to 'current satellites' but cites a DeepReef depth model, which is not a satellite product. The source does not support the claim as worded."}],"overall":"The report gives a sound reef-monitoring rationale and a usable Gantt chart, but it has no technical results and its central orbit choice (GEO at 600 km) is physically wrong. Software, information sources and contingencies are underdeveloped, and the schedule is inconsistent with the text.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":20.0,"max":40.0,"reasoning":"The rationale for reef monitoring is clear and footnoted, and the main users (marine and conservation scientists) are identified. Mission objectives are vague ('could also be used'), with no quantitative requirements such as resolution, revisit or coverage. Users beyond scientists (reef managers, tourism operators) are not developed. The stated orbit contradicts the mission goal, weakening the requirements picture. Referencing is URL footnotes rather than a reference list.","evidence":[{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","quote":"A near-real-time relay to monitoring stations on the ground would allow marine and conservation scientists"},{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","quote":"Hundreds of millions of humans rely on reefs for food, natural protection, jobs, and tourism."},{"where":"Project Plan & Preliminary Progress","quote":"My current choice is a geostationary orbit at an altitude of 600 km, which keeps the satellite fixed above the reef at all times."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":15.0,"max":30.0,"reasoning":"A Gantt chart with stages, status colours and deadlines gives a feasible plan. However, there are no explicit contingencies or variation provisions. The text says weeks 7 and 8 are for editing, but the chart places Editing & Finalisation in weeks 9 and 10. The plan is not tied to the mission narrative, and information sources are only implied rather than listed.","evidence":[{"where":"Project Plan & Preliminary Progress","quote":"Project work will be divided into weeks via a Gantt Chart."},{"where":"Project Plan & Preliminary Progress","quote":"Weeks 7 and 8 are reserved for review, amendments, final simulations, and reflection."},{"where":"Image 1","quote":""}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Deficient","mark":3.0,"scale":10.0,"points":3.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Progress is limited to reading a brief, background research and completing tutorials. No preliminary calculations, orbit parameters, STK outputs or figures are presented, so no technical results are shown. The only design decision stated (GEO at 600 km) is technically incorrect. The work shown does not demonstrate that the project is on track.","evidence":[{"where":"Project Plan & Preliminary Progress","quote":"completed STK tutorials 1, 2, 3 & 4"},{"where":"Project Plan & Preliminary Progress","quote":"More STK simulations needed."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":12.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Sections are clearly headed and the report reads reasonably well, with footnotes matched to superscripts. The Gantt chart has no figure caption or number and uses undefined grey and white cells. Some phrasing is awkward or informal ('maybe only one is required', 'More STK simulations needed'). The report lacks the software, information-sources and technical sections the brief asks for.","evidence":[{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","quote":"Oceans have become 30 percent more acidic since the industrial revolution leading to the bleaching of coral reefs."},{"where":"Project Plan & Preliminary Progress","quote":"More STK simulations needed."},{"where":"Image 1","quote":""}]}]}
